  "result": "I wrote the 30-minute lesson for O3, but I couldn't count its words. The shell command was blocked. I estimate about 3,200 visible words, roughly 21 minutes at 150 words a minute. That is inside the bench's 2,700 to 6,750 range but short of 4,500, so ask if you want it longer.\n\nThe lesson is `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. It opens with the win: for any piece of information in the makerspace assistant, say whether it belongs in the context window, in saved notes or in a searchable manual library, and why. The chunks are:\n- **Big picture:** the three places information can live.\n- **Context window and cost:** a worked example in which five steps send 26,250 input tokens in total, not the 8,450 of the final call. It also covers context rot.\n- **Between steps:** clearing old tool results, compaction, and keeping tool results small.\n- **Between sessions:** notes and the memory tool. It argues that safety facts like training status should come from a record your program owns, not a note the model wrote.\n- **Retrieval:** chunking, embeddings and BM25, contextual retrieval, and the \"skip retrieval if the manuals are under about 200,000 tokens\" check.\n- **Worked example:** a returning laser-cutter student, then a faded 3D-printer task for you to do.\n\nIt has four checks with immediate feedback and three figures. The sources are Anthropic's context engineering post (the primary one), the Claude docs on context windows and the memory tool, and the Contextual Retrieval post. I also wrote `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`, created `RESOURCES.md`, and updated `NOTES.md`.\n\nI found no community link I could verify, so the lesson names the Claude Developer Discord without a URL.\n\nYou can ask me anything about the lesson in the chat. A quiz is ready to write when you want one (`/quiz`).\n\nOne question for you: do you want the lesson longer, with more worked examples, or is this length right?",
